  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA deep 500mb trough centered over the eastern CONUS will gradually \nshift east this weekend. Meanwhile at the surface, high pressure \nwill sink south and become centered over the Midwest this weekend \nand then across the Ohio Valley early in the week. This will \nmaintain a chilly northerly flow across our region through Monday, \nalthough temperatures will gradually inch upwards a few degrees each \nday. As the surface high shifts east towards the Appalachians \nTuesday into Wednesday, southerly flow returns which boosts \ntemperatures back to near and then above normal heading into mid to \nlate next week.\n\nA prolonged dry period is expected through next Friday. \nPrecipitation this weekend will stay well south of our area as an \nupper low dives south from the Southern Plains into the lower \nMississippi Valley. As upper ridging builds across the Ohio Valley \nthrough Wednesday, shortwave energy will propagate through the north \ncentral CONUS but should stay well northwest of our area. The upper \nridge will then remain in place through the end of the week, \nresulting in a return of above normal temperatures. NBM \nprobabilities of hitting 70 degrees are above 50% by Friday, peaking \nin the 70-80% range by next Saturday. Guidance is in decent \nagreement bringing a cold front through the region next weekend, \nwhich would likely be accompanied by our next chance for \nprecipitation.",
